1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Mandar fishermen saw — a
fishing net.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
The Big Dipper. The Bugis see a ship.
Next door, the Mandar see a fishing net.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
The Mandar live on Sulawesi’s west
coast, facing the Makassar Strait. They
fish, and they trade.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Indonesian astronomers interviewed
retired fishermen, all over 70. Younger
ones no longer watch the stars.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The Bugis even call these two bowl stars
“the Mandar Widow.”

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,000
For the Mandar, those two sit on a boat
— not down the handle, but through Draco
to Boötes.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,000
The other five stars: three fishermen in
the middle, fishing gear at each end.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Old fishermen say: see it late at night
in April, and the cool season is near. A
fish called bambang appears.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Almost every recorded Mandar star
pattern belongs to the sea.

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The researchers note they only asked
coastal fishermen. No one has asked
inland rice farmers yet.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
In the north, look high. The Big Dipper
is one end of the net — all of it is up.

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
From Sulawesi, the net stays low over
the northern horizon.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The Big Dipper: one ladle, ten lives.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

